/*
================================================================================
QUERY TITLE      : Authentication Anomaly & Brute Force Detection
FILE NAME        : queries/01_authentication_anomalies.spl
AUTHOR           : Serah Kathini Mulwa
TARGET PLATFORM  : Splunk Enterprise / Splunk Cloud
TARGET INDEX     : cybersecurity_lab
SOURCETYPE       : _json
DATE CREATED     : 2026-11-08
STATUS           : Production / Portfolio Ready
================================================================================

OBJECTIVE & ANALYTICAL INTENT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
This threat-hunting SPL query scans normalized security telemetry to detect 
high-frequency authentication failure anomalies, potential brute-force attacks, 
and credential stuffing patterns originating from internal or external IPs.

It aggregates events across heterogeneous systems (Windows Security Events, 
Linux Syslog, Firewall sessions) by evaluating raw failure volume alongside 
the number of distinct targeted accounts within the search window.

KEY FIELDS EVALUATED:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- index                 : Scopes search to the isolated lab index (cybersecurity_lab).
- event.category        : Filters specifically for 'authentication' telemetry.
- event.action          : Identifies 'authentication_failure' outcomes.
- source.ip             : The originating IP address of the login attempt.
- user.name             : The targeted user identity / account name.
- host.name             : The target workstation, domain controller, or server.

ANALYTICAL INTERPRETATION & SOC WORKFLOW:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Threshold Trigger   : Flags any source IP exceeding 3 failed attempts (total_failures > 3).
2. Cardinality Analysis: Calculates distinct targeted accounts using dc(user.name).
   - High failures + 1 account  = Targeted Brute Force / Password Spray.
   - High failures + N accounts = Multi-Account Credential Stuffing.
3. False Positive Check: Single users mistyping passwords will produce low total_failures 
                         and dc(user.name)=1, filtering out noise.
================================================================================
*/

// STAGE 1: Scope search to lab index and filter for authentication failure events
index=cybersecurity_lab event.category="authentication" event.action="authentication_failure"

// STAGE 2: Perform statistical aggregation by originating source IP and target host
| stats 
    count as total_failures, 
    dc(user.name) as distinct_targeted_accounts, 
    values(user.name) as targeted_account_list,
    earliest(@timestamp) as first_observed_attempt,
    latest(@timestamp) as last_observed_attempt
    by source.ip, host.name

// STAGE 3: Apply post-aggregation anomaly threshold filter
| where total_failures > 3

// STAGE 4: Calculate duration delta in minutes between first and last attack event
| eval attack_duration_minutes = round((parse_datetime(last_observed_attempt, "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ") - parse_datetime(first_observed_attempt, "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")) / 60, 2)

// STAGE 5: Order results by failure magnitude to highlight high-risk threat actors
| sort - total_failures

// STAGE 6: Structure tabular output for SOC analyst review and dashboard rendering
| fields source.ip, host.name, total_failures, distinct_targeted_accounts, targeted_account_list, first_observed_attempt, last_observed_attempt, attack_duration_minutes
